this language is statically typed with an object oriented paradigm meant to compile to a language without arrays, and mutable strings

classes
 - can extend multiple classes, inheriting their members
 - when multiple members from different parents have the same name, it will throw a compile error
 - can be static, which prevents instantiating ths class
 - static classes can only have static members
 - final classes cannot be extended
 - omit the colon and parents if this is a root class
 - declaration syntax order: modifiers, identifier, colon, parents, and body

inheritors
 - inheritors implement abstract methods by overriding them with a implementation

members
 - can be protected, which only allows inheritors to access it
   private, which only allows this object to access it
   static, which only allows one instance of this property
 - members of the root are automatically static
 - members are public if there is no access modifier

functions refer to callables that are members of the root
containers refer to both callables and classes

methods
 - refers to callables within a class
 - when declared abstract, it wont have an implementation so calling it will throw a compile error
   declaring an abstract method in a non-static class wont throw a compile error
 - final methods cannot be overriden

final properties are constant values

callables
 - callable parameters can be pointers
 - when you dont want to return anything, omit the return type
 - declaration syntax order: modifiers, return type, identifier, parameters within parentheses, and the body

primitives
 - int
 - dec
 - bool
 - char
 - conststr
 - constarr

keywords
 - stat: for static
 - prot: for protected
 - priv: for private
 - abst: for abstract, only for methods
 - final: for final
 - class: for declaring a class
 - return: for returning in a callable

statements are seperated by semicolons
container bodies are contained with curly braces
parameters can be marked pointers by adding '&' before the class

identifiers
 - can only only numbers, letters, and underscores
 - cannot start with a number